<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_figure"><a href="#A4.F18" title="Figure 18In Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">18Decision boundaries of Mitra and baselines on 2D linearly separable data.</span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_figure"><a href="#A4.F19" title="Figure 19In Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">19Decision boundaries of Mitra and baselines on 2D nonlinear Gaussian mixture data.</span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_figure"><a href="#A4.F20" title="Figure 20In Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">20Decision boundaries of Mitra and baselines on 2D sine wave data.</span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_figure"><a href="#A4.F21" title="Figure 21In Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">21Decision boundaries of Mitra and baselines on 2D sinusoidal checkerboard data. Mitra shows more regular and less fragmented decision boundaries than TabPFNv2.</span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_figure"><a href="#A4.F22" title="Figure 22In Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">22Decision boundaries of Mitra and baselines on 2D spiral data.</span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_figure"><a href="#A4.F23" title="Figure 23In Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">23Decision boundaries of Mitra and baselines on 2D star data.</span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_figure"><a href="#A4.F24" title="Figure 24In Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">24Decision boundaries of Mitra and baselines on 2D Swiss roll data (from []).</span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_figure"><a href="#A4.F25" title="Figure 25In Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">25Large view of Figure , showing only the zoomed-in region for greater clarity.</span></a></li>
</ol></nav>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span><span id="A1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mitra</span></h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide details on the in-context learning (ICL) preliminaries, our data generation methods and priors, and the overall <span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mitra</span> algorithm.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>TFM Preliminaries</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To pretrain a TFM on purely synthetic data, each dataset <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(\mathbf{x}_{n},y_{n})\}_{n=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(\mathbf{x}_{n},y_{n})\}_{n=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from a prior distribution <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> that generates datasets with varying numbers of features, samples, classes (for classification tasks), and categorical attributes.
Once a TFM <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> has been pretrained, it can be used to perform ICL as follows. The model is given a support set <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{sup}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>sup</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{sup}}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=N_{\text{sup}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>sup</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=N_{\text{sup}}</annotation></semantics></math> labeled rows <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\mathbf{x}_{\text{sup}_{n}},y_{\text{sup}_{n}})\}_{n=1}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mtext>sup</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mtext>sup</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\mathbf{x}_{\text{sup}_{n}},y_{\text{sup}_{n}})\}_{n=1}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, along with <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=N_{\text{qry}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>qry</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=N_{\text{qry}}</annotation></semantics></math> unlabeled query rows <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{x}_{\text{qry}_{n}}\}_{n=1}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mtext>qry</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{x}_{\text{qry}_{n}}\}_{n=1}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s+q=N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s+q=N</annotation></semantics></math>. It then predicts the corresponding query labels <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{\text{qry}_{n}}\}_{n=1}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mtext>qry</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{\text{qry}_{n}}\}_{n=1}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> in a single forward pass:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{\text{qry}_{1}},\cdots,\hat{y}_{\text{qry}_{q}}=f_{\theta}([(\mathbf{x}_{\text{sup}_{1}},y_{\text{sup}_{1}}),\cdots,(\mathbf{x}_{\text{sup}_{s}},y_{\text{sup}_{s}}),\mathbf{x}_{\text{qry}_{1}},\cdots,\mathbf{x}_{\text{qry}_{q}}])," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mtext>qry</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mtext>qry</mtext><mi>q</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mtext>sup</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mtext>sup</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mtext>sup</mtext><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mtext>sup</mtext><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mtext>qry</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mtext>qry</mtext><mi>q</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{\text{qry}_{1}},\cdots,\hat{y}_{\text{qry}_{q}}=f_{\theta}([(\mathbf{x}_{\text{sup}_{1}},y_{\text{sup}_{1}}),\cdots,(\mathbf{x}_{\text{sup}_{s}},y_{\text{sup}_{s}}),\mathbf{x}_{\text{qry}_{1}},\cdots,\mathbf{x}_{\text{qry}_{q}}]),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">without the need to update its parameter <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Data Generation</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we discuss the specific parameters and modeling choices in the data generation for feature-target pairs <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{x},y)\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{x},y)\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and the details on the priors that we use in our data-generating mixture of priors in <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mitra</span>. For simplicity, Figure <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Target 𝑦. ‣ A.2.1 Feature and Target Generation ‣ A.2 Data Generation ‣ Appendix A Mitra ‣ Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows a visualization of a 2D dataset generated from our mixture of priors in <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mitra</span>. In addition, Figure <a href="#A1.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Target 𝑦. ‣ A.2.1 Feature and Target Generation ‣ A.2 Data Generation ‣ Appendix A Mitra ‣ Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows a t-SNE visualization of high-dimensional data samples from our mixture used during pretraining. We see both continuous and categorical features represented. The classification labels <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> are depicted in color.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.2.1 </span>Feature and Target Generation</h4>

<section id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Feature <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to include <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{cont}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cont</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{cont}}</annotation></semantics></math> continuous and <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{cat}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cat</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{cat}}</annotation></semantics></math> categorical components, such that <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{cont}}+N_{\text{cat}}=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cont</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cat</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{cont}}+N_{\text{cat}}=d</annotation></semantics></math>.
The number of categorical components is determined by <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{cat}}=\lfloor p_{\text{cat}}(d+1)\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cat</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>cat</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{cat}}=\lfloor p_{\text{cat}}(d+1)\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{cat}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>cat</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{cat}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a categorical percentage uniformly sampled.
We then uniformly sample the <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{cat}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cat</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{cat}}</annotation></semantics></math> categorical feature indices <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{\text{cat}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>cat</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{\text{cat}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}=\{1,\dots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=\{1,\dots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math> without replacement.
For each continuous feature index <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathcal{I}_{\text{cont}}=\mathcal{I}\setminus\mathcal{I}_{\text{cat}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>cont</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mtext>cat</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathcal{I}_{\text{cont}}=\mathcal{I}\setminus\mathcal{I}_{\text{cat}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we take <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> to be i.i.d. Gaussian noise.
For each categorical feature <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we generate its number of classes from a Geometric distribution. Lastly, we model each <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> via a multinomial distribution over its number of classes <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{c}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mtext>c</mtext><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{c}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Target <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each target <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, we handle its generation differently depending on whether the generating prior uses “direct” or “indirect” sampling.
For direct sampling, we directly simulate <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> from a random conditional distribution <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y\mid\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y\mid\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For indirect sampling, we first require fitting a classifier or regressor on a synthetically generated training dataset <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{x},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{x},y)</annotation></semantics></math> for the corresponding task.
In classification tasks, the label <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}\subset\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}\subset\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated similarly to the aforementioned label generating process for the categorical features.
In regression tasks, we take <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> to be normal. The final label <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated as the output from the fitted estimator on a new feature vector <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.21204v1/x_scatter_2D.png" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/470;" width="476" height="470" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="A1.F6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Randomly generated 2D Dataset from mixture of priors in <span id="A1.F6.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mitra</span><span id="A1.F6.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. The features <math id="A1.F6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> drawn from our mixture of SCM and TBP data-generating priors in <span id="A1.F6.6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mitra</span> on classification tasks. The classification labels <math id="A1.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> are shown in color.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.21204v1/x_scatter_tsne.png" id="A1.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/467;" width="476" height="467" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="A1.F7.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">t-SNE visualization of random high-dimensional dataset from mixture of priors in <span id="A1.F7.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mitra</span>.<span id="A1.F7.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The features <math id="A1.F7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> drawn from our mixture of SCM and TBP data-generating priors in <span id="A1.F7.6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mitra</span>. The classification labels <math id="A1.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> are shown in color.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.2.2 </span>Synthetic Data-Generating Priors</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We include a mixture of SCMs with TBPs, with both indirectly (ET, GB, DT, RF) and directly sampled priors (SCM, DSRF).</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Indirectly Sampled Priors.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ A.3 Algorithm ‣ Appendix A Mitra ‣ Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the data-generating procedure for “indirectly” sampled priors. We refer to these priors as indirectly sampled methods since they first require training data <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=[\mathbf{X},\mathbf{Y}]\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times(d+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=[\mathbf{X},\mathbf{Y}]\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times(d+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> to fit the estimator, i.e., classifier or regressor for the corresponding task, where <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the base size. Then, we generate features <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> according to the feature generation procedure described in Subsection <a href="#A1.SS2.SSS1" title="A.2.1 Feature and Target Generation ‣ A.2 Data Generation ‣ Appendix A Mitra ‣ Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2.1</span></a>. The final <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐘</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is output from the fitted estimator by predicting on this <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> input. We note that TabForestPFN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and Attic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> use an indirectly sampled DT prior, and TabICL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> uses an indirectly sampled XGBoost prior. Our data generation is similar to that in TabForestPFN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and Attic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> with the differences that we directly fit a Classifier for classification tasks rather than using a Regressor, and use our direct multinomial label generation procedure (see subsection <a href="#A1.SS2.SSS1" title="A.2.1 Feature and Target Generation ‣ A.2 Data Generation ‣ Appendix A Mitra ‣ Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2.1</span></a>) for both the target labels and categorical feature labels. Hence, we eliminate the need for using the quantile transform to bucketize the continuous values to labels. For these indirectly sampled TBPs, we use the classifiers and regressors from scikit-learn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Directly Sampled Priors.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#alg5" title="Algorithm 5 ‣ A.3 Algorithm ‣ Appendix A Mitra ‣ Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the data-generating procedure for our directly sampled random forest (DRSF) TBP prior. We refer to these priors as directly sampled because they first sample a function <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> from function space <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and then generate the targets <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{n}=f(\mathbf{x}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{n}=f(\mathbf{x}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for data <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, directly sampled methods only need to form <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> once and then the directly-sampled data-generating priors directly output <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐘</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For DSRF, we generate the features <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> using the same feature generation process from Subsection <a href="#A1.SS2.SSS1" title="A.2.1 Feature and Target Generation ‣ A.2 Data Generation ‣ Appendix A Mitra ‣ Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2.1</span></a>. For DSRF, we must first construct random trees from <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Algorithm  <a href="#alg4" title="Algorithm 4 ‣ A.3 Algorithm ‣ Appendix A Mitra ‣ Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). To do so, we sample the following: (1) random split indices in <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,\dots d-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,\dots d-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the feature dimension; (2) random split thresholds in a specified range thres-int. Each node in the tree stores its corresponding split index and split threshold. We store the split intervals in a dictionary to track the sub-intervals corresponding to the feature split index to sample from on future splits as the algorithm progresses. We follow the convention from DTs, where the tree is split on a feature index <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and value <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, and all datapoints such that <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}[i]\leq v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}[i]\leq v</annotation></semantics></math>, are split to the left side of the tree and those such that <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}[i]&gt;v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}[i]&gt;v</annotation></semantics></math> are on the right side of the tree. We also control the number of nodes with no children. We construct the leaf node labels differently depending on the task type. For classification, we uniformly randomly sample a starting index in <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,\dots,N_{c}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,\dots,N_{c}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of classes, and we increment it for each subsequent leaf node added modulo the number of classes. For regression, we sample the leaf nodes from a Gaussian distribution. We sample number of estimators (random trees) <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we traverse each tree until a leaf node is reached to get a target value for that tree (see Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ A.3 Algorithm ‣ Appendix A Mitra ‣ Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). Lastly, we compute the final label <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> using majority voting over these <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> values for classification tasks and using the mean for regression tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For details on the SCM prior, see Appendix C.1 of the TabPFN paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Algorithm</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm  <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ A.3 Algorithm ‣ Appendix A Mitra ‣ Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides an overview of our <span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mitra</span> method.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The population version of the likelihood in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Pretraining TFM on Prior Mixture ‣ 3 Mitra: TFM Pretrained on a Mixture of Priors ‣ Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> takes a form of an expectation over the table <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, with each table sampled from the prior mixture. Given the sampled table, the model assumes <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> query rows are conditionally independent given the in-context examples, so that the log-likelihood within the expectation is decomposed into a sum of <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> individual terms, one per query row. When the query label corresponds to a classification task, its (conditional) distribution is assumed to be categorical, making the training objective equivalent to minimizing the cross-entropy loss. When the query label corresponds to a regression task, its (conditional) distribution is assumed to be Gaussian and the training objective corresponds to minimizing the Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> <span id="alg1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mitra</span>: Mixture of Synthetic Priors for pretraining TFMs</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Set of data generators <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{G}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{G}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, with mixture weights <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{w_{i}\}_{i=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{w_{i}\}_{i=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{M}w_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{M}w_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>,

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            Support set size <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, query set size <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, number of pretraining steps <math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
              Sample generator index <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\sim w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\sim w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,M</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              Sample synthetic dataset <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{(i)}=\{(\mathbf{x}_{n}^{(i)},y_{n}^{(i)})\}_{n=1}^{N_{i}}\leftarrow\mathcal{G}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{(i)}=\{(\mathbf{x}_{n}^{(i)},y_{n}^{(i)})\}_{n=1}^{N_{i}}\leftarrow\mathcal{G}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
              Randomly partition <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> into support set <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{(i)}_{\text{sup}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>sup</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{(i)}_{\text{sup}}</annotation></semantics></math> and query set <math id="alg1.l6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{qry}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>qry</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{qry}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="alg1.l6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{D}^{(i)}_{\text{sup}}|=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>sup</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{D}^{(i)}_{\text{sup}}|=s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{D}^{(i)}_{\text{qry}}|=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>qry</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{D}^{(i)}_{\text{qry}}|=q</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              Construct input sequence: <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[(\mathbf{x}^{(i)}_{\text{sup}_{1}},y^{(i)}_{\text{sup}_{1}}),\ldots,(\mathbf{x}^{(i)}_{\text{sup}_{s}},y^{(i)}_{\text{sup}_{s}}),\mathbf{x}^{(i)}_{\text{qry}_{1}},\ldots,\mathbf{x}^{(i)}_{\text{qry}_{q}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mtext>sup</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><msub><mtext>sup</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mtext>sup</mtext><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><msub><mtext>sup</mtext><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mtext>qry</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mtext>qry</mtext><mi>q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[(\mathbf{x}^{(i)}_{\text{sup}_{1}},y^{(i)}_{\text{sup}_{1}}),\ldots,(\mathbf{x}^{(i)}_{\text{sup}_{s}},y^{(i)}_{\text{sup}_{s}}),\mathbf{x}^{(i)}_{\text{qry}_{1}},\ldots,\mathbf{x}^{(i)}_{\text{qry}_{q}}]</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
              Predict query labels via TFM: <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{y}^{(i)}_{\text{qry}_{1:q}}=f_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mtext>qry</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}^{(i)}_{\text{qry}_{1:q}}=f_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
              Compute loss: <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\log p_{\theta}(y^{(i)}_{\text{qry}_{1:q}}|\mathcal{D}^{(i)}_{\text{sup}},\mathbf{x}^{(i)}_{\text{qry}_{1:q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mtext>qry</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>sup</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mtext>qry</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\log p_{\theta}(y^{(i)}_{\text{qry}_{1:q}}|\mathcal{D}^{(i)}_{\text{sup}},\mathbf{x}^{(i)}_{\text{qry}_{1:q}})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
              Update model parameters <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> using gradient of <math id="alg1.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Indirectly Sampled TBPs.</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Task generator <span id="alg2.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TBP</span>, base size <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, feature dimension <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, number of samples <math id="alg2.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, number of classes <math id="alg2.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, task type <math id="alg2.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}\in\{\textsc{Classification},\textsc{Regression}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Classification</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Regression</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}\in\{\textsc{Classification},\textsc{Regression}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            Generate training data <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}=[\mathbf{X},\mathbf{Y}]\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times(d+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}=[\mathbf{X},\mathbf{Y}]\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times(d+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="alg2.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=[\mathbf{x}_{1};\dots;\mathbf{x}_{B}]\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=[\mathbf{x}_{1};\dots;\mathbf{x}_{B}]\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg2.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}=[y_{1},\dots,y_{B}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>B</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}=[y_{1},\dots,y_{B}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, according to the procedure in Subsection <a href="#A1.SS2.SSS1" title="A.2.1 Feature and Target Generation ‣ A.2 Data Generation ‣ Appendix A Mitra ‣ Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2.1</span></a>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            Instantiate model <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\leftarrow\begin{cases}\texttt{TBPClassifier},&amp;\text{if }\mathcal{T}=\textsc{Classification};\\
\texttt{TBPRegressor},&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">TBPClassifier</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Classification</mtext></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">TBPRegressor</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\leftarrow\begin{cases}\texttt{TBPClassifier},&amp;\text{if }\mathcal{T}=\textsc{Classification};\\
\texttt{TBPRegressor},&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            Fit model: <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z.\texttt{fit}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">fit</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z.\texttt{fit}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{Y})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
            Sample testing features <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
            Generate predictions: <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}_{2}\leftarrow z.\texttt{predict}(\mathbf{X}_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐘</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">predict</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}_{2}\leftarrow z.\texttt{predict}(\mathbf{X}_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbf{X}_{2},\mathbf{Y}_{2}]\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times(d+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐘</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{X}_{2},\mathbf{Y}_{2}]\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times(d+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<figure id="alg3" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 3</span> </span> Target Generation From DT Traversal</figcaption>
<div id="alg3.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg3.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg3.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">function</span> <span id="alg3.l1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DT-Traversal</span>(<math id="alg3.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="alg3.l1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tree</span>)

</div>
<div id="alg3.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
